module Core

import Native

{-
   Reading a float - the inverse of `Show.yana` beside this, and the third file of the subject.

   `Ryu.yana` says why the three are here and why none of them is a module of its own. The scan is
   shared and the conversion is not: finding where a number ends and pulling its digits apart is text
   work with one answer on both targets, and turning those digits into the nearest float is Ryu's
   inverse natively and the host's `Number` on JS.

   The `Read` class, the error type and the integer instances are `Core/Read.yana`'s. This joins them
   by declaring instances, which needs no import and no `pub` - a module is a directory.
-}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Reading a float.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The scan is shared and the conversion is not. Finding where a number ends and pulling its digits
   apart is text work with one answer on both targets; turning those digits into the nearest float
   is Ryu's inverse natively and the host's `Number` on JS, for the reason Host gives.
-}

-- What a decimal number in the text turned out to be. `special` is 0 for an ordinary one, 1 for an
-- infinity and 2 for a NaN, which are the two `show` writes and which therefore have to read back.
data Scanned {stop: Size, mantissa: U64, exponent: Int, digits: Int, negative: Bool, special: Int}

fn lowerAscii(unit: Int) -> Int = if unit >= 65 && unit <= 90 then unit + 32 else unit

{-
   The extent and the pieces of a decimal number at `from`, or nothing where there is not one.

   **Seventeen digits are kept and the rest are counted.** Seventeen is what a double distinguishes,
   so an eighteenth changes nothing about the answer - but its *place value* does, and dropping a
   digit divides the mantissa by ten. Without the matching increment of the exponent the whole
   number reads as a tenth of itself, which is a bug this library has had before and which is why
   the sentence is here rather than in a commit message.

   The kept digits are rounded on the first one dropped rather than truncated, which keeps the
   error below half a unit in the seventeenth digit - well inside one unit in the last place of the
   result. It is not the exact rounding a big-integer fallback would give; it is what keeps a literal
   written with more digits than a double holds from landing on the wrong side of its neighbour.
-}
fn scanDecimal(text: String, from: Size) -> Maybe(Scanned):
    let units = length(text)
    let &at = from
    let &negative = False

    if at < units:
        let unit = String.unit(text, at)
        if unit == 45:
            negative = True
            at = at + 1
        else if unit == 43:
            at = at + 1

    -- `Inf` and `NaN`, which are what `show` writes and are matched in either case. Three units and
    -- no more: `Infinity` reads as `Inf` with the cursor left on the `i`, which is the same answer
    -- a caller gets for any trailing text it did not ask about.
    if at + 3 <= units:
        let first = lowerAscii(String.unit(text, at))
        let second = lowerAscii(String.unit(text, at + 1))
        let third = lowerAscii(String.unit(text, at + 2))

        if first == 105 && second == 110 && third == 102:
            return Just(Scanned {stop: at + 3, mantissa: 0, exponent: 0, digits: 0,
                                 negative: negative, special: 1})

        if first == 110 && second == 97 && third == 110:
            return Just(Scanned {stop: at + 3, mantissa: 0, exponent: 0, digits: 0,
                                 negative: negative, special: 2})

    let &mantissa = 0 :: U64
    let &digits = 0 :: Int
    let &dropped = 0 :: Int
    let &fractional = 0 :: Int
    let &roundUp = False
    let &afterPoint = False
    let &any = False

    let &eight = 0 :: U64

    while at < units:
        {-
           Eight digits at a time, where the kept mantissa has room for eight more.

           `digits <= 9` is the whole guard and it is exact: seventeen is what a double distinguishes,
           so a block may only be taken while nine or fewer have been kept. It is therefore entered at
           most twice - one to nine, nine to seventeen - and a value written with more digits than
           that reaches the dropped-digit arm below a unit at a time, which is where it belongs since
           each of those changes the exponent and not the mantissa.

           `digits > 0` keeps the leading zeros out of it. They are places rather than digits, so a
           block taken across them would count eight of something this loop deliberately does not
           count. Core/Read.yana's `eightMoreDigits` is the load, and it answers `False` on JS.
        -}
        while digits > 0 && digits <= 9 && eightMoreDigits(text, at, eight):
            mantissa = mantissa * 100000000 + eight
            digits = digits + 8
            if afterPoint then fractional = fractional + 8
            at = at + 8

        if at >= units then break

        let unit = String.unit(text, at)

        if unit == 46:
            if afterPoint then break
            afterPoint = True
        else:
            let digit = digitValue(unit, 10)
            if digit < 0 then break

            any = True

            if digits < 17:
                mantissa = mantissa * 10 + wideCount(digit)

                -- Leading zeros are not significant digits, which is what makes `0.00012` five
                -- digits' worth of room rather than one.
                if mantissa != 0 then digits = digits + 1
            else:
                if dropped == 0 && digit >= 5 then roundUp = True
                dropped = dropped + 1

            if afterPoint then fractional = fractional + 1

        at = at + 1

    if !any then return Nothing

    let &exponent = 0 :: Int

    if at < units:
        let unit = String.unit(text, at)

        if unit == 101 || unit == 69:
            let &probe = at + 1
            let &negativeExponent = False

            if probe < units:
                let sign = String.unit(text, probe)
                if sign == 45:
                    negativeExponent = True
                    probe = probe + 1
                else if sign == 43:
                    probe = probe + 1

            let &value = 0 :: Int
            let &length = 0 :: Int
            let &sawDigit = False

            while probe < units:
                let digit = digitValue(String.unit(text, probe), 10)
                if digit < 0 then break

                sawDigit = True

                -- Four digits is past every exponent either width has, and the cap is what keeps a
                -- written one from wrapping the `Int` it is accumulated in.
                if length < 4:
                    value = value * 10 + digit
                    if value != 0 then length = length + 1

                probe = probe + 1

            -- An `e` with no digits after it is not part of the number, so the cursor stays in front
            -- of it and `1e` reads as `1`.
            if sawDigit:
                exponent = if negativeExponent then 0 - value else value
                at = probe

    if roundUp then mantissa = mantissa + 1

    return Just(Scanned {stop: at, mantissa: mantissa, exponent: exponent - fractional + dropped,
                         digits: digits, negative: negative, special: 0})

@platform(native) let infinityBits = 9218868437227405312 :: U64
@platform(native) fn signBitOf(negative: Bool) -> U64 =
    if negative then (1 :: U64) `shl` wideCount(63) else 0

{-
   Ryu's inverse - `FloatRead.cpp`, and the same tables run the other way.

   `mantissa * 10^exponent` is turned into `m2 * 2^e2` by one multiply against the table, with
   `exact` recording whether that multiply lost anything; the rounding at the end needs to know,
   because a value exactly halfway between two floats rounds to even and a value merely *near* the
   half rounds to whichever side it is on.
-}
@platform(native) fn convertDouble(mantissa: U64, exponent: Int, digits: Int, negative: Bool) -> Double:
    let sign = signBitOf(negative)

    -- Below 1e-324 there is no float to round to but zero, and above 1e309 none but an infinity.
    -- Both keep the sign, which is what makes `-1e-400` a negative zero.
    if mantissa == 0 || digits + exponent <= 0 - 324 then return bitcast(sign) :: Double
    if digits + exponent >= 310 then return bitcast(sign `or` infinityBits) :: Double

    let highest = (truncate(bitWidth(mantissa)) :: Int) - 1
    let &e2 = 0 :: Int
    let &m2 = 0 :: U64
    let &exact = False

    if exponent >= 0:
        let power = log2Pow5(exponent)
        e2 = highest + exponent + power - 53
        m2 = mulShiftPow5(mantissa, exponent, e2 - exponent - (power + 1) + 125)

        -- The multiply is exact when 2^e2 divides the product, which it does whenever `e2` is below
        -- `exponent` and otherwise exactly when the mantissa has that many trailing zero bits.
        exact = e2 < exponent || (e2 - exponent < 64 && multipleOfPowerOf2(mantissa, e2 - exponent))
    else:
        let power = log2Pow5(0 - exponent)
        e2 = highest + exponent - (power + 1) - 53
        m2 = mulShiftInv(mantissa, 0 - exponent, e2 - exponent + (power + 1) - 1 + 125)
        exact = multipleOfPowerOf5(mantissa, 0 - exponent)

    let &ieeeExponent = e2 + 1023 + ((truncate(bitWidth(m2)) :: Int) - 1)
    if ieeeExponent < 0 then ieeeExponent = 0
    if ieeeExponent > 2046 then return bitcast(sign `or` infinityBits) :: Double

    -- How far `m2` has to come down to be a 52-bit field. A subnormal is the case the `1` covers:
    -- its exponent field is zero and its value is scaled as though the field were one.
    let shift = (if ieeeExponent == 0 then 1 else ieeeExponent) - e2 - 1023 - 52

    -- Past the width of the value there is nothing left to keep, which is a number below the
    -- smallest subnormal - the bound above admits a few of those, and this is their answer.
    if shift >= 64 then return bitcast(sign) :: Double

    let &field = m2
    let &roundUp = False

    if shift > 0:
        let stillExact = exact && bitsUpTo(m2, wideCount(shift - 1)) == 0
        let lastRemoved = (m2 `shr` wideCount(shift - 1)) `and` 1

        -- Up when the bit below the field is set *and* either something survives under it or the
        -- field would otherwise be odd, which is round-half-to-even said in bits.
        roundUp = lastRemoved != 0 && (!stillExact || ((m2 `shr` wideCount(shift)) `and` 1) != 0)
        field = (m2 `shr` wideCount(shift)) + carryOf(roundUp)

    field = bitsUpTo(field, 52)

    -- A field that carried out of its top is the next exponent's zero field, and the exponent has
    -- room: the largest one this can reach is the infinity, whose field is zero as well.
    if field == 0 && roundUp then ieeeExponent = ieeeExponent + 1

    return bitcast(sign `or` (wideCount(ieeeExponent) `shl` wideCount(52)) `or` field) :: Double

@platform(native) fn scannedDouble(text: String, from: Size, scanned: Scanned) -> Double:
    if scanned.special == 2 then return 0.0 / 0.0
    if scanned.special == 1:
        let infinite = bitcast(infinityBits) :: Double
        return if scanned.negative then 0.0 - infinite else infinite

    return convertDouble(scanned.mantissa, scanned.exponent, scanned.digits, scanned.negative)

{-
   And the host's own, which is `Number` handed the number the *scan* produced.

   **Rebuilt from the mantissa and the exponent rather than sliced out of the source**, which is the
   one decision here and is about the two targets agreeing rather than about this one. `Number` is
   correctly rounded over the whole decimal; `convertDouble` is correctly rounded over the seventeen
   digits it was given, and the scan is where the eighteenth was dropped. Handing the host the
   original text would make it read digits the other target never saw, and the two would answer one
   unit in the last place apart for every input written with more digits than a double holds - which
   is exactly the input a program is most likely to be surprised by.

   So the approximation lives in one place, in the scan, and both targets convert the same number.
-}
@platform(js) fn scannedDouble(text: String, from: Size, scanned: Scanned) -> Double:
    if scanned.special == 2 then return 0.0 / 0.0
    if scanned.special == 1:
        let infinite = 1.0 / 0.0
        return if scanned.negative then 0.0 - infinite else infinite

    let &digits = String.ofCapacity(32 :: Size)
    pushUnsigned(scanned.mantissa, digits)
    String.pushUnit(digits, 101)

    if scanned.exponent < 0:
        String.pushUnit(digits, 45)
        pushUnsigned(wideCount(0 - scanned.exponent), digits)
    else:
        pushUnsigned(wideCount(scanned.exponent), digits)

    let magnitude = hostToNumber(digits)
    return if scanned.negative then 0.0 - magnitude else magnitude

{-
   `Read(Double)` and `Read(Float)` - the inverse of the two instances above.

   `Read(Float)` reads a double and narrows it, which is *exactly* rounded rather than nearly so:
   double rounding is safe when the intermediate carries at least `2p + 2` bits of the target's
   precision, and 53 is comfortably above the 50 a `Float`'s 24 bits ask for. That is a theorem
   rather than a measurement, and it is why there is no second table here.
-}
instance Read(Double):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Double) = match scanDecimal(from, at):
        Just(scanned):
            let value = scannedDouble(from, at, scanned)
            at = scanned.stop
            Ok(value)

        Nothing -> Err(NoDigits)

instance Read(Float):
    fn read(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, Float) = match scanDecimal(from, at):
        Just(scanned):
            let value = scannedDouble(from, at, scanned)
            at = scanned.stop
            Ok(truncate(value) :: Float)

        Nothing -> Err(NoDigits)
